1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,085
Hello, students. You have heard this one.

2
00:00:02,125 --> 00:00:06,293
It would take a person thousands of years
just to read what the model was trained on.

3
00:00:06,333 --> 00:00:08,168
That sentence is not a measurement.

4
00:00:08,208 --> 00:00:11,392
It is a division, and somebody
left one of the numbers out.

5
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:13,418
Here is the whole thing in one breath.

6
00:00:13,458 --> 00:00:16,043
A scale slogan is not a
measurement, it is a division —

7
00:00:16,083 --> 00:00:20,850
and the input that moves the answer
most is usually the one nobody states.

8
00:00:21,083 --> 00:00:22,918
Start where the numbers are real.

9
00:00:22,958 --> 00:00:26,710
GPT-3 is the anchor here, because its
details were actually published —

10
00:00:26,750 --> 00:00:28,918
a hundred seventy-five billion parameters,

11
00:00:28,958 --> 00:00:31,168
roughly three hundred
billion training tokens,

12
00:00:31,208 --> 00:00:35,433
and a reported three point one four times
ten to the twenty-third operations.

13
00:00:35,667 --> 00:00:39,350
Everything you have heard about years of
reading is built on that middle number.

14
00:00:39,417 --> 00:00:40,418
So run it yourself.

15
00:00:40,458 --> 00:00:44,460
Three hundred billion tokens, at about
zero point seven five words per token,

16
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:47,043
is two hundred twenty-five billion words.

17
00:00:47,083 --> 00:00:49,168
Read nonstop at two hundred
fifty words a minute,

18
00:00:49,208 --> 00:00:51,585
and you get one thousand
seven hundred eleven years.

19
00:00:51,625 --> 00:00:52,877
There is the headline.

20
00:00:52,917 --> 00:00:55,918
Now notice what just happened — two
of those lines were assumptions,

21
00:00:55,958 --> 00:00:57,017
not measurements.

22
00:00:57,250 --> 00:00:59,210
Now move the one nobody stated.

23
00:00:59,250 --> 00:01:00,460
At two hundred words a minute,

24
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:03,835
the same pile of text takes two
thousand one hundred thirty-nine years.

25
00:01:03,875 --> 00:01:06,627
At a hundred fifty, two thousand
eight hundred fifty-two.

26
00:01:06,667 --> 00:01:09,210
Same numerator, same
model, same sentence —

27
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:11,085
and eleven hundred
forty-one years of spread,

28
00:01:11,125 --> 00:01:14,433
entirely from a reading
speed no one ever supplied.

29
00:01:14,583 --> 00:01:16,543
Here is where the cynical read goes wrong.

30
00:01:16,583 --> 00:01:18,460
The comparison is not useless.

31
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:20,543
Every version of it says
the same true thing —

32
00:01:20,583 --> 00:01:23,460
more text than a person could
read in many lifetimes.

33
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:25,793
That claim survives
whichever rate you pick.

34
00:01:25,833 --> 00:01:28,127
What does not survive
is the decimal place.

35
00:01:28,167 --> 00:01:31,558
It is an illustration with a
hidden parameter, not a finding.

36
00:01:31,792 --> 00:01:34,752
The compute figure behaves
exactly the same way.

37
00:01:34,792 --> 00:01:37,960
Suppose you could do a billion
operations per second, by hand.

38
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:42,835
GPT-3's reported training budget would take
you about nine point nine five million years.

39
00:01:42,875 --> 00:01:47,085
Same shape as before — a published number
on top, an invented human underneath,

40
00:01:47,125 --> 00:01:49,350
and a headline falling
out of the division.

41
00:01:49,625 --> 00:01:51,585
And this is why the habit earns its keep.

42
00:01:51,625 --> 00:01:53,710
You may have heard over a
hundred million years instead.

43
00:01:53,750 --> 00:01:58,085
That claim needs about three point two
times ten to the twenty-fourth operations —

44
00:01:58,125 --> 00:02:00,502
roughly ten times GPT-3's budget.

45
00:02:00,542 --> 00:02:02,210
Plausible for a later system.

46
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:04,127
But it is a different claim
about a different model,

47
00:02:04,167 --> 00:02:07,850
and a reader who cannot tell them
apart cannot audit either one.

48
00:02:07,958 --> 00:02:10,293
So here is the move,
stripped of this example.

49
00:02:10,333 --> 00:02:11,585
Take the impressive number.

50
00:02:11,625 --> 00:02:13,918
Write it out as the
division it actually is.

51
00:02:13,958 --> 00:02:17,002
Mark every line that is an assumption
rather than a measurement.

52
00:02:17,042 --> 00:02:19,085
Then change the one that was never stated.

53
00:02:19,125 --> 00:02:21,960
If the answer moves a lot, you
are holding an illustration,

54
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,183
not a finding.

55
00:02:23,458 --> 00:02:27,085
Verdict. What this chapter gets right is
refusing to let a slogan stand in for

56
00:02:27,125 --> 00:02:29,960
arithmetic — it hands you the
script and the recorded output,

57
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,543
so you can re-run the
whole thing yourself.

58
00:02:32,583 --> 00:02:35,293
Where it bites is that this
is a habit, not a fact.

59
00:02:35,333 --> 00:02:37,585
Nobody can hand you the
list of hidden parameters.

60
00:02:37,625 --> 00:02:39,517
You go looking, every time.

61
00:02:39,583 --> 00:02:42,543
Before you go, here is what
this video does not establish.

62
00:02:42,583 --> 00:02:46,335
Nothing here shows that zero point seven
five, or any particular reading rate,

63
00:02:46,375 --> 00:02:47,793
is the right value.

64
00:02:47,833 --> 00:02:50,543
I took GPT-3's published figures on trust.

65
00:02:50,583 --> 00:02:55,168
All I showed you is how far the answer
moves when an input nobody stated changes.

66
00:02:55,208 --> 00:02:57,183
Sensitivity is not accuracy.

67
00:02:57,417 --> 00:02:58,835
Your turn. Here is the prompt.

68
00:02:58,875 --> 00:03:02,210
Take an impressive number from my own
field and restate it as the division it

69
00:03:02,250 --> 00:03:06,793
actually is. List every input, mark which
ones are measured and which are assumed,

70
00:03:06,833 --> 00:03:11,252
then show me how far the answer moves across
a reasonable range for each assumption.

71
00:03:11,292 --> 00:03:13,558
That last clause is the whole thing.

72
00:03:13,792 --> 00:03:16,960
Do not ask whether the number is
right — ask how far it can move.

73
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,460
If it swings by a factor of two, you
have found the hidden parameter.

74
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:22,752
Run it on a number you
have repeated in public.

75
00:03:22,792 --> 00:03:26,002
This video was created by
Ethan Gomes from INFO 7375.

76
00:03:26,042 --> 00:03:28,725
Thanks for watching, students.
